        "date":  "2026-09-24",
        "theme":  "Orchestration",
        "summary":  "The article proposes an Agent Development Life Cycle framework that extends traditional software development practices to address the unique governance challenges of building autonomous AI systems. It covers essential stages including agent definition, design, evaluation, observability, boundary enforcement, and governance. An agent control plane is recommended to maintain consistency, policy enforcement, and oversight across multiple agents and teams at enterprise scale.",
        "execSummary":  "The Agent Development Life Cycle framework fills a genuine gap: most enterprises are deploying AI agents using software engineering workflows that were not designed for autonomous systems, creating invisible governance blind spots around boundary enforcement and observability. The agent control plane concept — a centralized layer ensuring policy consistency across all agents — is the most operationally actionable recommendation for enterprises scaling beyond pilot deployments. For customer support and operations leaders, the practical implication is that agent sprawl without a control plane will produce the same audit and compliance failures that unmanaged SaaS adoption created a decade ago. The key caveat is that this framework is prescriptive and vendor-agnostic, which means implementation responsibility falls entirely on the enterprise\u0027s platform and architecture teams.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
