        "summary":  "The article argues that AI security requires defined requirements, enforceable controls, and named accountability across the full agent stack rather than relying on agent reasoning alone. Organizations must implement security boundaries independent of agent behavior, including identity management, access controls, and continuous testing. The piece advocates for open research and shared tools to help more defenders meet security standards by learning from collective failures.",
        "execSummary":  "This piece reframes AI agent security as an engineering discipline with clear ownership requirements — a message that directly challenges the common enterprise assumption that prompt engineering or model alignment is sufficient protection for agentic deployments. The argument that security controls must be enforced at infrastructure layers independent of the agent\u0027s reasoning is operationally actionable: it maps to existing zero-trust and defense-in-depth frameworks that enterprise security teams already understand. For leaders building or procuring agentic systems, the practical implication is that AI security cannot be delegated to the AI vendor — it requires the same layer-by-layer control verification applied to any critical system. The NVIDIA platform lean is light here; the content is largely framework-neutral and applicable across agent stacks.",
